From 1e0e7a718215527bd3ea423c80f5029565e708e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Cursor Agent <cursoragent@cursor.com>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 12:38:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] docs(deploy): cover tgi-process and instance records on
 Kubernetes

The Kubernetes section only described vllm-process; it predates the
tgi-process launcher and the instance record file.

It now covers both process launchers: an image with vLLM or
text-generation-launcher, the FORGEMESH_VLLM_* / FORGEMESH_TGI_* bind
and advertise settings, model downloads, and a memory-backed /dev/shm
for instances that span several GPUs, since NCCL and PyTorch may need
more shared memory than the 64 MiB a container gets by default.

It also explains the record file on Kubernetes. The file sits next to
the database on the data volume, which has to stay writable because a
failed write fails the instance start. The records never stop anything
after a container restart there: the control plane is PID 1 of its
container, so the kernel ends the instances even when it is killed,
and the restarted control plane finds none of the recorded processes
(it only signals a process whose pid and start time both match) and
drops the records. The text does not rely on the PID namespace check:
on kind the restarted container got the same pid:[...] identifier as
the killed one, so the records were dropped by the pid and start time
check rather than ignored as a whole.

The manifest notes the files kept next to FORGEMESH_DB_PATH, and the
launcher policy header names tgi-process.
